    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Yes, it adheres to concrete. No, that does not make it a concrete product. The distinction sounds pedantic right up until the moment a driveway-sized coat of liquid rubber starts lifting in sheets the following spring.
        </p>

        <h2 id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. The Short Answer
        </h2>

        <p>
          Flex Seal bonds to concrete and holds well on <strong>small, contained, low-traffic repairs</strong>: a cracked planter, a leaking concrete pond, the gap around a pipe passing through a wall, a hairline crack you need to stop weeping until a proper repair kit arrives. In those roles it does exactly what it advertises.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is the wrong product for <strong>anything large, walked on, driven on, or below grade</strong>. Driveways, garage floors, patios, basement walls and slabs all fail it for the same underlying reasons: it is a mechanically-adhered rubber film with limited abrasion resistance, applied to a material that constantly transmits moisture from the ground up.
        </p>

        <p>
          The rest of this page is why, because knowing the mechanism tells you which of your own concrete jobs fall on which side of that line — and because the same reasoning applies to every rubberised coating sold for masonry, not just this one brand.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Where It Genuinely Works on Concrete
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Planters, Ponds and Water Features</h3>
        <p>
          A concrete planter or a small pond is close to ideal: contained, low pressure, no traffic, and a surface that benefits from a flexible coating because the vessel expands and contracts with temperature. Clean and dry it thoroughly, build the coating up in thin layers rather than one thick pass, and give it the full cure before refilling. Where fish or plants are involved, check the manufacturer's guidance before you start rather than after.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Pipe and Conduit Penetrations</h3>
        <p>
          Where a pipe passes through a concrete wall, the annular gap is small, defined and mechanically stable — the best possible case for a flexible sealant. Brushed on, it holds for years. This is arguably the single most sensible use of the product anywhere in a house.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Emergency Stops</h3>
        <p>
          Water coming through a crack on a Saturday night, with the injection kit days away, is a legitimate reason to reach for a rubberised patch. The mistake is not using it there — it is letting the temporary fix quietly become the permanent one.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Concrete Adjacent to Metal or Plastic</h3>
        <p>
          Where a repair spans two materials — a metal flashing meeting a concrete step, a plastic drain fitting set into a slab — the ability to bond to both at once is genuinely useful, and few dedicated concrete products manage it.
        </p>

        <!-- Comparison Table -->
