                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Patio slab</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">No</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Silane-siloxane penetrating sealer</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Damp basement wall</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">No</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Cementitious waterproofer, two coats</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="where-it-fails" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Where It Fails, and Why
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Anything With Traffic on It</h3>
        <p>
          Cured liquid rubber is soft. That flexibility is the whole point of the product, and it is exactly wrong for a surface people walk on or drive over. Shoe grit scuffs it, tyres scrub it — particularly when a car turns while stationary — and hot tyres in summer can lift it outright. Garage floor coatings exist as a separate category precisely because this abrasion problem needed solving with different chemistry.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Anything Large</h3>
        <p>
          Flex Seal is priced for patching. Coating a driveway with a product sold in spray cans and quarts costs several times what a purpose-made driveway sealer costs per square foot, and buys a coating with no traction rating, no UV formulation for that scale, and no warranty for the application. Even if the performance were equal, the arithmetic would not be.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Anything Below Grade</h3>
        <p>
          On a basement wall or a slab in contact with soil, water pressure pushes moisture from the wet side toward the dry side. A film adhering mechanically to the dry face has nothing but surface grip resisting that, and the bond fails from behind rather than the front. We cover that failure in detail in <a href="/flex-seal-for-basement-walls/">Flex Seal for basement walls</a>, and the underlying chemistry in <a href="/drylok-vs-flex-seal/">DRYLOK vs Flex Seal</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Anything Under Long UV Exposure</h3>
        <p>
          Rubberised coatings on a horizontal surface in full sun take an ultraviolet beating that a patch on a gutter seam never sees. Chalking, hardening and cracking follow, and on a large area the failure is visible long before it is complete.
        </p>

        <h2 id="vapour" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The Problem Nobody Mentions: Vapour Drive
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the mechanism behind most coating failures on concrete, and it is worth understanding because it applies far beyond one brand.
        </p>

        <p>
          Concrete in contact with the ground is never truly dry. Moisture moves continuously upward through the slab by capillary action and evaporates from the surface — a process that goes unnoticed until something impermeable is placed on top of it. A rubber film is impermeable by design. Moisture arriving at the underside of that film cannot evaporate, so it accumulates, and the pressure it exerts is enough to break a mechanical bond and lift the coating in blisters.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is why a garage floor that appears bone dry can still reject a coating, why patio slabs on grade lift film-forming products in patches, and why penetrating sealers — which line the pores and leave the concrete breathable — are the default recommendation for exterior slabs. A vapour barrier under a slab reduces the problem in newer construction; almost no older slab has one.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical test costs nothing: tape a two-foot square of plastic sheet tightly to the concrete and leave it overnight. Condensation underneath in the morning means moisture is moving up through the slab, and any impermeable coating applied there is on borrowed time.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Cost Comparison Nobody Runs</h3>
        <p>
          It is worth putting numbers on the scale problem, because the per-can price hides it. A quart of brush-on liquid rubber covers a very small area at the thickness it needs to work — realistically a few square feet per coat, and it wants two. A gallon of purpose-made concrete or driveway sealer covers a couple of hundred square feet per coat and costs less than a couple of those quarts.
        </p>
        <p>
          On a modest 400-square-foot patio, that is the difference between a job costing tens of dollars in sealer and one costing several hundred in patch product — for a coating that is less suited to the surface, carries no traction rating, and will need removing before anything better can go on. Even setting performance aside entirely, the arithmetic alone rules it out at that scale.
        </p>

        <h2 id="instead" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. What to Use Instead
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Weatherproofing a patio, path or slab.</strong> A penetrating silane-siloxane sealer. It soaks into the pores, repels water from within, leaves nothing on the surface to peel or go slippery, and lets the slab breathe. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> covers the field, and <a href="/sealing-old-concrete/">sealing old concrete</a> covers preparation on a weathered slab.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A driveway.</strong> An acrylic or penetrating driveway sealer sized and priced for the area, applied with a squeegee or roller. Our <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">driveway sealer reviews</a> cover both surfaces.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A garage floor.</strong> Epoxy or polyaspartic, which are formulated for abrasion, hot tyres and chemical spills. See our <a href="/best-epoxy-garage-floor-kit/">epoxy garage floor kit guide</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Cracks.</strong> Hydraulic cement for a static crack, a self-levelling polyurethane crack filler for a slab joint, or an injection kit for an actively leaking crack in a poured wall — see <a href="/driveway-crack-repair/">driveway crack repair</a> and <a href="/basement-crack-repair/">basement crack repair</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A damp basement wall.</strong> A cementitious waterproofer in two coats on bare, prepared masonry, or an elastomeric membrane if the wall moves.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Steps and thresholds.</strong> A worn concrete step that needs both repair and traction wants a resurfacer or a purpose-made step-repair mortar rather than a coating over the damage. Whatever goes on top afterwards should be matte and, ideally, carry a slip-resistant additive — a step is the one place on a property where a slippery finish is genuinely dangerous rather than merely annoying.
        </p>

        <h2 id="already-applied" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. If You Have Already Used It
        </h2>

        <p>
          If the coating is <strong>sound and holding</strong> — no blisters, no lifting, no damp edges — leave it. A small patch that is working is not worth disturbing, and plenty of pipe penetrations and planters sealed this way last for years.
        </p>

        <p>
          If it is <strong>blistering or peeling</strong>, do not recoat over it. The bond has already failed and a second layer fails the same way, faster. Take the loose material off with a scraper and work out where the moisture is coming from.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you want to <strong>treat the concrete properly</strong>, all of the rubber has to go first. Nothing cementitious, no stain and no penetrating sealer will bond through it. Scraping lifts the loose sheets; the well-bonded remainder needs a grinder with a cup wheel and dust extraction. Wear proper respiratory protection — silica dust is a genuine hazard, not a nuisance — and expect this to take considerably longer than applying the coating did.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Patch Product at Slab Scale</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The scale mismatch is the root error. Flex Seal is formulated and priced for defined repairs; a driveway or patio is a surface. Ask it to be a surface coating and it fails from behind at several times the cost of the right product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Moisture Coming Up Through the Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dry-looking slab on grade still transmits vapour. Tape down plastic sheet overnight before committing to any impermeable coating — condensation underneath means the coating will blister.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying to a Damp or Dusty Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mechanical adhesion needs a clean, dry, sound surface. Concrete dust in particular acts as a release layer, so vacuum and wipe rather than sweeping and hoping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting a Stopgap Become the Repair</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Patching a leaking crack to get through a storm is sensible. Leaving that patch in place means water keeps travelling through the wall while the evidence stays hidden — and in a freezing climate the damage behind it continues.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming You Can Seal or Stain Over It Later</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nothing that needs to bond with concrete will bond through rubber. Using it on a surface you may want to seal, stain or coat properly converts a simple job into a grinding project first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="the-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">Flex Seal on Concrete: The Verdict</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-green-400">Use it if:</strong> the job is small, contained and out of the way — a cracked planter, a concrete pond, the gap around a pipe, or an emergency patch to buy a few days. On those, it works and there is often nothing better at that size.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Do not use it if:</strong> the concrete is walked on, driven on, below grade, or larger than a few square feet. Those failures are structural to how the product works, not a matter of applying it more carefully.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> Flex Seal works on concrete in the same way a plaster works on a cut — genuinely, on the right size of problem. Concrete's real jobs are sealing a slab, coating a floor, filling a crack and waterproofing a wall, and each of those has a product category built for it that costs less and lasts longer. Keep a can for the planter and the pipe, and buy the right thing for everything else.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Flex Seal work on concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">It bonds to concrete and holds on small contained repairs — a cracked planter, a pond wall, a pipe penetration. It is not suitable for driveways, garage floors, patios or any large slab, because it is a mechanically-adhered film with little abrasion resistance and moisture pushing up through the concrete will lift it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use it on a concrete driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. A driveway is large, trafficked, UV-exposed and transmitting moisture from below — the worst combination for a rubberised patch coating. It scuffs under tyres, lifts where vapour pushes from beneath, and costs several times more per square foot than a proper driveway sealer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Will it stop a crack leaking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Temporarily. It bridges the surface while water keeps moving through the full thickness of the crack, and pressure behind the patch eventually pushes it off. A polyurethane injection kit fills the crack front to back and is the lasting repair in a poured wall.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it last on concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">On dry, clean, stable concrete with no pressure behind it and no traffic over it, several years. On a below-grade wall or a slab with moisture moving through it, often a single wet season — the film is pushed off from behind rather than worn from the front.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal or stain over it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Not with anything that needs to bond to the concrete. Cementitious waterproofers, stains and penetrating sealers all require direct masonry contact, and rubber blocks it. Removing it means scraping and grinding — the main reason not to use it where you may want a proper treatment later.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does it work on a concrete pond?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">This is one of its better concrete uses — contained, low pressure, no traffic, and flexibility helps as the vessel expands and contracts. Clean and dry thoroughly, build up thin coats, and allow the full cure before refilling. Check the product guidance where fish or plants are involved.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What should you use instead?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Match product to job: penetrating silane-siloxane for weatherproofing slabs, a driveway sealer for drives, hydraulic cement or polyurethane injection for cracks, epoxy or polyaspartic for garage floors, and a cementitious waterproofer for damp basement walls.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to walk on?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">It is not designed as a walking surface — the cured rubber has little abrasion resistance, scuffs under foot traffic and can be slippery when wet. Anything walked on regularly needs a coating formulated for traffic with a slip-resistant finish.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

